A||N||6||jaguiler||Rubber plug boots -  high spped handling - 2 questions||||5||
Z||000000||jaguiler||03-22-2001||08:17 PM||||Hello Everyone once again,<P>I was trying to change my plugs on my v6.  On the plugs by the firewall I am having trouble removing the wire boot from the plug. I would like to change my wires too...<BR>I tried pulling and pulling on them, is there a tool to help me take these off?  The ones by the trunk were difficult too...but I was able to get them off....<P>2nd question - at high speeds -- 75+ the car seems to be shakey, a littkle squirrelly... it gives me an uneasy feeling.  I just passed Maryland inspections so I know the suspension components are good.. the tires are pretty new as well....  I know the shocks only have about 16K miles on them.  Is this the way a Fiero supposed to handle?  BTW it is an '86 GT ~ I know other cars handle this way but I figured the GT would feel pretty stable at those speeds... or am I just spoiled by my other car?<P>Thanks!<P><P>------------------<BR>______<BR>  |aguiler<BR>\/||65.8.46.102||reg||5
Z||000001||MrPBody||03-22-2001||08:30 PM||||Shouldn't be squirrelly; my '85 SE is quite stable at highway+ speeds.  "Responsive", yes, but not "squirrelly".<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||03-22-2001||08:54 PM||||I would keep in mind that just because you passed a state inspection doesn't mean your car is perfect.  Here in NC the quaility of the inspection varies GREATLY from shop to shop.  Also since they can only charge about $18 and that includes a smog check, most shops that do inspections usually do a quick visual brake and light check, tire tread check and off you go.  <P>You could easily have worn control arm bushings or ball joints that would not be identified by the typical state inspection.  I would jack the car up and do a complete visual inspection of all suspension components.  Shake everything and see if you can find some excess movement of tie rods, control arms, ball joints, and wheel bearings.  Tire pressure and condition of the tires can also affect the handling.<P>I am having a severe handling problem with my blue 87 GT.  It doesn't track well at all, wandering all over the road when driving over ruts in the pavement.  The last alignment I had done was done very poorly and they left the right side caster was out of spec by several deg.  I plan to check the engine mounts and cradle bushings carefully and might replace the rear struts before I take it to another alignment shop to get it aligned correctly.<P>It should handle well at almost any speed.  Good luck tracking it down.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-22-2001||08:59 PM||||If you want the car to handle, just start replacing the bushings with urethane right now.  Sooner or later, you're gonna' do it so just start now.<P>The first set to swap out are the rear control arm bushings.<P>Also, replace the rear axle nuts.  You need a 30mm socket for this.  Pep boys sells this stuff, as would most auto parts chain stores.  This is a good job for an impact gun, as the nuts are designed to be hard to turn.  Use your spare tire to lower the car and still provide access to the nut.  <P>Also, if the front wheels jiggle put in new front wheel bearings.  They are not the most robust bearings, so if they are showing signs of wear just replace them.<P>Also, get a new steering damper. It will really help.  Please let me say again a new steering damper will really help with high speed stability.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||LottaBallsCamaro||03-22-2001||10:36 PM||||Do fieros have a rack and pinion(as opposed to a recirculating ball), If so you rack can have air in it. when a power steering pump suck air it will pump down into the rack and create slack. Did fieros even come with power steering, I know my friends doesnt.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LPR||03-22-2001||10:51 PM||||Fiero's don't come w/ power steering...<P>I had a hard time with my wires too.  Be carfull not to pull to hard because if they do come off when your pullen' on em real hard you could hit your hand on the lid like I did!  Sometimes the boot can come off and the wire will stay on, so beware of that tooo, I don't know of a tool for it<BR>luc||||reg||12
Z||000006||2birds||03-23-2001||09:14 PM||||I solved the broken plug boot problem by getting a set of Taylor Spiro-Pro wires from Summit Racing.  Under $40, and I haven't broken one in 7 years.  The red ones match the engine graphics on a V-6 nicely, too.  They have a nice spot to grip them, and you don't need the boot heat shields any more.<P>Lots of things can cause twitchy (that's how I describe mine) handling.  I attribute mine to crappy old I-95 in Florida.  It has grooves from the big trucks, and I don't think it has been repaved in 30 years.  Wider tires (I have 215/60/15 on my front end) amplify this.  Mine has all polyurethane, complete ball joint replacement 6 years ago, and a very meticulous alignment, courtesy of the fine folks at Sears (They practically let me do it myself).  It is still very twitchy on rough roads at high speeds, so I just learn to live with it.  It keeps you awake, for sure.||||reg||1
